{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/1503587-is-there-anything-stopping-a-fresh-arisen-from-utterly-flipping-out","id":1503587,"title":"Is there anything stopping a fresh Arisen from utterly flipping out?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-02-20T18:43:53-08:00","id":1503587,"title":"Is there anything stopping a fresh Arisen from utterly flipping out?","content":"I stumbled upon <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/nmummy-is-there-anything-stopping-a-fresh-arisen-from-utterly-flipping-out.685182/\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> ancient thread raising the question of what prevents mummies from immediately breaking out the magic nukes and turning the setting into something completely different. I don't think they came up with very good answers before they got off-topic, but it's been a decade since then, so I was curious if the community had better answers. I'm not much of a Mummy player, but I'm still curious.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13248-jsnider3","name":"jsnider3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-20T19:40:28-08:00","id":1503590,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>jsnider3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/1503587-is-there-anything-stopping-a-fresh-arisen-from-utterly-flipping-out#post1503587\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I stumbled upon <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/nmummy-is-there-anything-stopping-a-fresh-arisen-from-utterly-flipping-out.685182/\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> ancient thread raising the question of what prevents mummies from immediately breaking out the magic nukes and turning the setting into something completely different. I don't think they came up with very good answers before they got off-topic, but it's been a decade since then, so I was curious if the community had better answers. I'm not much of a Mummy player, but I'm still curious.</div> </div> </div> </div>The strictest answer is &quot;Nothing.&quot; The cult, the Guild members above and equal to them, and even the Judges or other high-end entities can take action against them for doing it, but unless you have someone able to do something about it immediately on the scene, there's very little most character types can do, and the one's capable of handling it are usually not going to be burning as bright as a newly Arisen mummy.<br /> <br /> Of course, some logic needs be applied, first being that this can, in some ways, be more of a blessing than a curse. The highest scale of damaging Utterances are there to be <i>used</i>, after all, and the narrative has ready explanations for anyone who lets loose with such to start with. THe Arisen operate on a timer, and many of the people who oppose their general goals of shaping civilization, hiding from punishment, hoarding relics, etc., have had a lot of time to setup a large number of barriers that would be a pain in the ass to deal with. Going bombastic creates a general environment where it's easier for the Arisen to complete their tasks in the time where they have the most power, and if they're particularly smart about, turn right around into a cult recruitment tool, as the cult and their holdings are more likely to survive the resulting action and to have resources for assisting victims of the disasters, and thus create a lot of good will and opportunity to create connections and even severely interest people into indoctrination.<br /> <br /> Now for the actual caveats you were probably expecting. While these powers are intended to be used and storytellers should be willing to remember and accommodate that, players should be willing to keep in mind the setting and the things that'll matter to the characters as well as their play group, including the Storyteller. Open dialogue between players is expected, and good will should be accorded around. So a total and complete devastation of a city is unliekly unless everyone at the table is on board.<br /> <br /> Next up is the mechanical consideration, which basically boils down to &quot;It takes particular arrangements to have a full blown third tier Utterance, and those arrangements basically can only be used once from the go.&quot; The costs in general are too high to make happen for most starting characters with only nine Pillar dots to spread around. More experienced Arisen on different Descents may not have that problem, but the player probably have different priorities at that point. Sure, a henet of Arisen can each unleash a whole string of disasters, but individual Arisen really aren't going to get more than one off before they need to recover Pillars, and by the time they have, they're probably down a few Sekhem, since it burns out so quickly in the beginning.<br /> <br /> Which brings us around to in-game considerations. Going nuts when you wake up due to theft by unleashing a disaster can actually make it harder to retrieve the stolen relic, given how it can end up buried or disappearing into the earth in any number of ways. Cult summons tend to have directive are better facilitated by the setting remaining standing after the fact, and the overall directive of Arisen to shape civilization, not destroy it, is oft better accommodated if civilization remains to be shaped. The Judges have every right to strike an Arisen down for excessive damage that goes beyond their goals, the Arisen themselves have as much reason to <i>not</i> do that as <i>to</i> do it, and given that, after the opening scene, they know they have goals and a timeframe, it pays to hold their cards until they know when destruction of that sort is most optimal. That goes double since those Utterances are basically lighting a signal flare for anyone with any amount of magical sense, and the scale invites the strongest of them to get their retribution vis a vie overkill, which is definitely a problem when you have shit to do. So on and so forth.<br /> <br /> To keep it simple, what keeps the Arisen from doing it? Effectively, the question of if doing it at all actually does anything for them. Again, these are powers that are meant to be used, but all of their powers* are meant to be used <i>to an end</i>. They apply surprisingly often for their area of work, but there are cielings and floors, and there comes a point where being dumb about it doesn't help anything.<br /> <br /> As for why the setting doesn't resemble some of the other settings more because of that, mostly it comes down to the fact that magic and the supernatural are pretty universally actively harmful to the setting of humanity, and on the other side of things the Powers That Be have an investment in the status quo.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Oh yeah, and stuff like that mixed in with the Arisen's Timelessness nature means that precogs are gonna have ample opportunity to set up what they can, for whatever that amounts to.<br /> <br /> *Well, okay, it's canon that the Arisen weren't supposed to learn Utterances, and that this last gift from Sutek was intended as a wrinkle to the schemes of the Judges and the Shan'iatu. Hasn't stopped them from being incorporated into the grand scheme.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 02-20-2023, 11:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}